<HEAD><style>/* Classic Editorial — identity is hierarchy, spacing, grid, and proportion. Every value below
   is a theme variable; nothing depends on a face, a hue, or a texture. */
.gml-card {
  --hairline: var(--neutral-border-color);
  --grid-divider-color: var(--hairline);
  --grid-divider-width: 1px;
}

/* 1 · Kicker over a hairline: the tracked-caps eyebrow carries its own full-width hairline as a
   bottom border, so the pair is one node and the heading below keeps the native eyebrow hug.
   Dose: one per card, top of the column it introduces. */
.kicker.gml-paragraph { padding-bottom: 7pt; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); margin-block-end: 10pt; }
/* Heavy rule: the masthead / act-break register in heading ink. Dose: covers, dividers, closing. */
.heavy.gml-divider { --divider-color: var(--heading-color); --divider-thickness: 2pt; border-radius: 0; }

/* 2 · Stat rail and metric: tabular figures so stacked numbers align; the gloss hugs its figure. */
.metric { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums; }
.metric h2.gml-heading, .metric h3.gml-heading, .metric .gml-title { letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
.gloss.gml-paragraph { margin-block-start: calc(var(--block-gap) * 0.3); }
/* Inside a metric rail the gloss sits directly under its figure: the figure's own descent supplies the air. */
.metric .gloss.gml-paragraph { margin-block-start: 0; }

/* Column takeaway under a metric rail: the concluding claim stands off from the last ruled row by
   more than the rows keep between themselves, so it reads as the column's conclusion rather than a fourth figure. */
.claim.gml-paragraph { margin-block-start: calc(var(--block-gap) * 2.5); }

/* 3 · Two-column measure: sustained prose at 55–70 characters per line, ragged right, no seam. */
.prose p.gml-paragraph { text-wrap: pretty; hyphens: manual; }
.measure { max-width: 520pt; }
/* Masthead headline (cover, closing): nine columns, so a 60-character headline wraps to two lines. */
.measure.wide { max-width: 660pt; text-wrap: balance; }

/* 5 · Accent quote glyph and quote voice: one oversized opening mark set as a display node beside
   the quote; the quote itself reads in the heading voice with no bar. */
.quote.gml-title { line-height: 0.6; margin-block-end: 0; }
.quote.gml-blockquote { padding-inline-start: 0; }
.quote.gml-blockquote > .gml-blockquote__bar { display: none; }
.quote.gml-blockquote p.gml-paragraph { font-family: var(--heading-font); font-weight: var(--heading-font-weight); line-height: 1.12; letter-spacing: -0.015em; }

/* 6 · Figure caption grammar: a semibold lead-in, a muted sentence, then a credit/source line. */
.source.gml-paragraph { margin-block-start: calc(var(--block-gap) * 0.45); }

/* 7 · Ruled ledger: caps header over a heavy rule, hairlines between rows, numerals right-set. */
.ledger { --table-border-color: var(--hairline); --table-border-width: 1px; --table-header-color: var(--heading-color); --table-header-weight: 600; --table-cell-padding: 12pt 12pt 12pt 0; --table-radius: 0; }
.ledger .gml-table__row[data-header] .gml-table__cell { border-bottom: 2pt solid var(--heading-color); letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: calc(11 * var(--type-anchor, 1pt)); }
.ledger .gml-table__cell:not([data-col='0']) { text-align: end; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
.ledger .gml-table__row:last-child .gml-table__cell { border-bottom: var(--table-border-width) solid var(--table-border-color); }

/* 8 · Numbered column head: a light large numeral over a hairline, drawn by the grid marker so it
   renumbers on reorder. Dose: peer columns and steps. */
.tick .gml-grid-cell { --marker-color: var(--heading-color); --marker-font-weight: 300; --marker-font-size: calc(var(--role-display-md-pt, 52) * var(--type-anchor, 1pt)); --marker-rule-color: var(--hairline); --marker-rule-thickness: 1px; }

/* Contents rows: muted inline ordinals in a narrow gutter. */
.toc .gml-grid-cell { --marker-color: var(--body-color-muted); --marker-font-weight: 400; --marker-inline-size: 30pt; --marker-gap: 10pt; }

/* Photographic plates keep square corners — the register has no rounded frames. A caption or name
   set under an in-flow plate keeps a clear line of air from the crop (≥14pt). */
.plate .gml-figure, .plate .gml-figure__img { border-radius: 0; }
.plate .gloss.gml-paragraph, .plate h4.gml-heading { margin-block-start: 15pt; }
/* People cells: the role line hugs its name (6pt) and the credit sentence stands off from the role (16pt),
   so name+role read as one group and the sentence as the next. */
.plate .meta.gml-paragraph { margin-block-start: -5pt; margin-block-end: 7pt; }

/* Cover metadata (byline, date, folio-style credits): tracked caps at caption size. */
.cover-meta.gml-paragraph { letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }

/* Icon chips on the icon-led list: one outlined circle treatment, an outlined circle over each column head. */
.chips .gml-grid-cell { --marker-border-color: var(--heading-color); --marker-border-width: 1px; --marker-color: var(--heading-color); --marker-gap: 10pt; }</style></HEAD>
<section image-layout="blank" id="6ushZY_0Zd"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="TP5WlI_ukD"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">MEDIA EVOLUTION · VISUAL COMMUNICATION</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><display size="lg" class="measure wide">The page changed its behavior</display><p font-size="lg" class="measure">The page did not disappear. It moved from fixed instance, to coded interactive state, to generated variation.</p><h3 role="subheading" class="measure">When a medium changes, production, distribution, interaction, and authorship are reorganized.</h3><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="caption" class="cover-meta">THESIS · MEDIA EVOLUTION REORGANIZES ROLES; IT DOES NOT GUARANTEE IMPROVEMENT.</p><hr class="heavy"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="jBqINRC3zm"><grid divider="sm neutral-border"><grid-item id="9s7iVRP-Jc" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">A WORKING DEFINITION</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><h1>A medium is a system</h1><p>Print stabilizes a version. Digital systems update and respond. Computational systems generate and vary.</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="caption">Transformation begins when these relationships change—not when a surface merely looks new.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="VB7LaP1mxK" colspan="40"><grid marker="icon" marker-size="large" marker-style="outline" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill" class="chips"><grid-item id="S7HoBTL600" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/5B6i5D877HhdTptwsV8NJQ.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="typographic composing tools and file formats" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;An Adobe Acrobat PDF file icon, consisting of a white page with a black trapezoid shape, symbolizing a document or file in the Portable Document Format.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Tools and formats</h3><p>Shape what can be made and what counts as a finished form.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="wx2rQxI_r5" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/gEfcKBPruKx1cEXelEfF.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="distribution infrastructure connected routes" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;An arch shape formed by two leaning lines that connect to a horizontal line on top, embodying the concept of infrastructure.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Infrastructure</h3><p>Determines how work circulates, persists, and reaches an audience.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="LbPlS3HtpO" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/9ouIQHYODGMzf0CAm1BR.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="interactive interface cursor and screen" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A monitor screen in a simple form, conveying the concept of display or screen.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Interfaces</h3><p>Frame perception and define the actions available to a user.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="KN5HRnZlyD" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/b9cAMkUS6R5F5mSXxYQ9.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="shared conventions people participating" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A single solid black figure with no distinguishing features, set against a white background, symbolizing an individual taking part in an activity or event.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Conventions</h3><p>Organize participation, interpretation, and expectations of authorship.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="08_LI7nOXR" card-margin-hidden="true"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="uc0ka6ai31" class="plate" padding="0" colspan="34"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/Y6LAGzNcT9qISSC_hwji2.png" saved-media-id="kezdffdpg3vttaq" source="image.ai-generated" fill="true" image-style="documentary-feature" alt="Documentary reportage photograph inside a contemporary working letterpress shop, a printer locking separate mirrored metal type into a rectangular chase on a composing stone, quoins and spacing material visible around the forme, candid mid-distance standing-eye-level vantage, available window light, true-to-life neutral color, honest workshop depth, no posed face, no logos, no readable type or signage" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"></grid-item><grid-item id="nIRZEao4R7" class="prose" colspan="26"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">PRINT CIVILIZATION</p><h2>Print stabilizes space</h2><p>Print turns composition into a repeatable spatial decision. Type, margins, sequence, and edition stabilize hierarchy across copies. Revision happens before circulation; the reader mainly encounters a finished arrangement.</p><p><b>Object lesson:</b> movable type makes the logic physical—separate, mirrored units must be selected, spaced, and locked into a grid before ink reaches paper.</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="caption" class="source">Generated documentary-style view of a contemporary letterpress workshop; conceptual illustration, not archival evidence.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="-FNfapk_vG"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="Fsi9VAhGBV"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">DIGITAL ERA · DOCUMENTED MILESTONES</p><h2>The document joins a network</h2><p class="measure">The page becomes addressable, linkable, updateable, and connected to user action. Publishing shifts from distributing finished copies to maintaining networked versions.</p><grid divider="sm neutral-border" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="5bN_NkahG2" colspan="38"><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><smart-layout variant="minimalTimelineWithBoxes" orientation="horizontal" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>1989</h4><p>Tim Berners-Lee proposes the Web at CERN.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>30 April 1993</h4><p>CERN places Web software in the public domain.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><spacer mode="fill"></spacer></grid-item><grid-item id="TdV-sYo56C" class="prose" colspan="22"><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="eyebrow">THE CHANGED RELATIONSHIP</p><h3>From reader to user</h3><p>A coded state can respond, link elsewhere, and change after publication. The designer now structures pathways and behavior as well as composition.</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer></grid-item></grid><p role="caption" class="source">Source: <a href="https://home.cern/science/computing/the-birth-of-the-web/">CERN, “The birth of the Web”</a>.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="RpWjBRqe-F"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="Dmeqb-n5Cj"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">COMPARATIVE LENS</p><h2>What changes across the three eras?</h2><table col-widths="[22,26,26,26]" header-row="true" borders="rows" striped="false" class="ledger"><tbody><tr><td><p>Dimension</p></td><td><p>Print</p></td><td><p>Digital</p></td><td><p>Computational</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Production</b></p></td><td><p>Composed fixed instance</p></td><td><p>Maintained coded state</p></td><td><p>Generated variation</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Distribution</b></p></td><td><p>Editions</p></td><td><p>Network access</p></td><td><p>On-demand synthesis</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Audience</b></p></td><td><p>Reader</p></td><td><p>User</p></td><td><p>Participant and evaluator</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Authorship</b></p></td><td><p>Visible makers</p></td><td><p>Collaborative stacks</p></td><td><p>People, data, models, and interfaces</p></td></tr></tbody></table><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="caption" class="source">Dominant tendencies, not sealed eras: each newer system carries older forms inside it.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="eAdq1zG09V"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="IMAvnOaHi7"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">AGE OF COMPUTATIONAL INTELLIGENCE</p><h2>Computational intelligence makes the output variable</h2><p class="measure">The shift is from resolving one artifact to designing the conditions from which many possible artifacts can emerge.</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><smart-layout variant="cycle" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>Intent and constraints</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Data, rules, or prompt</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Model computation</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Variable output</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Human judgment</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Revision</h4></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="caption">Scale and variation increase; provenance, opacity, bias, and evaluation become design problems inside the loop—not external technical details.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="VtUmZ_XYjR"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="0_kcBlBYYr"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">TRADE-OFFS, NOT DESTINY</p><h2>Progress is not a staircase</h2><p class="measure">Each system expands some capacities while constraining others. The relevant question is not “Which era wins?” but “Which conditions fit the purpose and consequence?”</p><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="u2NCLcFSnb" border="lg solid" border-sides="top" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">PRINT</p><h3>Stable and inspectable</h3><p>Composition holds across copies, but revision is slower and circulation remains physical.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="oQDteM_GhK" border="lg solid" border-sides="top" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">DIGITAL</p><h3>Accessible and responsive</h3><p>Interaction and rapid updates grow alongside dependence, volatility, and distraction.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="0ZxIsg6Ir-" border="lg solid" border-sides="top" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">COMPUTATIONAL</p><h3>Variable and scalable</h3><p>Generation multiplies outputs while introducing opacity, homogenization, and uncertain provenance.</p></grid-item></grid><hr class="heavy"><h3>Media accumulate rather than replace one another. Designers combine systems to match purpose, audience, and consequence.</h3></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="vI9JNh84E4"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="IDaMx_sPZa"><p role="eyebrow" class="kicker">A METHOD FOR PRACTICE</p><h2>Design the conditions, not only the artifact</h2><grid divider="sm neutral-border" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="RFg_sLHoZC" colspan="38"><p role="eyebrow">READ ANY MEDIUM THROUGH FIVE QUESTIONS</p><grid divider="sm neutral-border" direction="vertical" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="Y5s9O8BiJ_" vertical-align="center"><h4>What is fixed, and what can vary?</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="u-GH-eCQrM" vertical-align="center"><h4>Who can make, publish, revise, and respond?</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="xUr9qDPkH0" vertical-align="center"><h4>How does the work travel?</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="Jbl9mipChE" vertical-align="center"><h4>Where are decisions visible or hidden?</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="TRUCnkD0Ng" vertical-align="center"><h4>What does the interface ask the audience to become?</h4></grid-item></grid></grid-item><grid-item id="9s3w1o9r8N" background-color="surface" colspan="22"><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="eyebrow">STUDIO PROMPT</p><display size="md">One message.<br>Three systems.</display><p>Translate one message across print, interactive, and generative forms. Keep the intent constant and document how production, distribution, interaction, and authorship change.</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><hr class="heavy"><p role="caption">Evaluate the changed roles—not only the changed appearance.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>